MVP Scope

Quvvatla’s first MVP is intended to validate the practical feasibility and market demand for a mobile EV charging service in Uzbekistan.

The MVP is built around one central principle:

EV charging should adapt to people’s lives — not the other way around.

The first MVP will focus on testing whether mobile EV charging can provide a more convenient, flexible, and time-efficient charging experience for EV users.

User Problem

EV owners may need to spend personal time searching for available charging stations, travelling to charging locations, checking availability, waiting for an available charging slot, and organizing their schedules around the charging process.

As EV adoption grows, convenience and time become increasingly important parts of the charging experience.

Quvvatla aims to reduce this burden by bringing the charging service closer to where and when the customer needs it.

The goal is to allow customers to spend less of their personal time managing vehicle charging and more time on what matters to them — family, work, health, sport, leisure, and everyday life.

Core Value Proposition

Quvvatla is not intended simply to provide another charging point.

The project is designed to create a service-based mobile EV charging experience centered on customer convenience, flexibility, and time saving.

Instead of requiring the customer to organize their day around access to a fixed charging location, the service concept is designed to make charging more flexible and better integrated into the customer’s daily routine.

MVP Objectives

The MVP is intended to validate:

real customer demand for mobile EV charging;
the value customers place on convenience and time saving;
priority customer segments and use cases;
practical service scenarios;
operational feasibility;
service accessibility and reliability;
customer experience;
repeat demand;
requirements for future scale.

Initial Service Scope

The first MVP is expected to include:

a limited mobile EV charging service;
a basic customer request process;
service scheduling and coordination;
charging session execution;
operational monitoring;
customer feedback collection;
pilot performance data collection.

Initial Customer Segments

Potential early customer segments may include:

private EV users;
corporate fleets;
logistics and transportation operators;
commercial parking operators;
roadside assistance providers.

The pilot will help determine which customer segments experience the strongest need for flexible charging and place the greatest value on convenience and time saving.

Validation Areas

The MVP will be used to evaluate:

frequency of charging requests;
customer time-saving value;
service accessibility;
response time expectations;
operational reliability;
customer satisfaction;
repeat demand;
priority use cases;
scalability potential.

Out of Scope for the Public Repository

This public repository intentionally does not disclose:

detailed technical architecture;
energy system specifications;
charging equipment specifications;
supplier and technology partner information;
pricing methodology;
unit economics;
proprietary operational processes;
detailed software architecture;
implementation logic.

These elements remain confidential and will be developed separately as Quvvatla progresses toward MVP implementation.
